# Sectoria Design System Rules

Design language: Modern Enterprise Minimalism + Bento Grid.
Reference: Sectoria_Design_System.md (full spec). This rule file
enforces the non-negotiable constraints — consult the full spec
document for measurements, values, and reasoning.

## Token discipline

- EVERY color, spacing, radius, shadow, duration value must come
  from a token defined in `packages/ui/src/theme.css` @theme block.
  Never write a hex value, px value, or rgba() inline in a component.
  If a token doesn't exist for what you need, add it to theme.css FIRST,
  then use the token.
- Two fonts only: `var(--font-sans)` (Inter) for all UI text,
  `var(--font-mono)` (JetBrains Mono) for all data: CNIC, NTN, PKR
  amounts in tables, serial codes, certificate numbers, CPR refs.
  No third font under any circumstance.
- Spacing uses the 4px base grid. Legal values: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24,
  32, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96px. Any other value needs explicit justification
  in a code comment before it's accepted.

## Color rules

- Colors are never decorative. Every color token encodes information
  (status, interaction state, hierarchy). If you're reaching for a
  color to "make it look nice," stop — use whitespace and typography
  weight instead.
- Semantic colors are reserved for semantic use only:
  --color-success → verified, compliant, complete
  --color-warning → pending, awaiting, late
  --color-danger  → blocked, rejected, non-filer, error
  --color-info    → in-progress, informational
  Never repurpose a semantic color for a non-semantic visual purpose.
- Status badges: always colored dot + colored text on tinted background.
  Color alone (background only, no dot or text) is never sufficient.

## Bento grid

- Use for: society comparison sections, dashboard metric areas,
  feature sections on the public marketplace, compliance overviews.
- One anchor cell (2×2) per bento section maximum — two equal anchors
  is a two-column hero, not a bento.
- Gutter = half of inner cell padding. Default: 16px gutter, 24px
  inner padding. Never mix different gutter values within one grid.
- Cell importance = cell size. Don't size cells by convenience or
  available space — size by what the content importance warrants.

## Component requirements

- Every status badge: dot + text + tinted background (see Section 5.3
  of the design spec). Never text alone on a colored background.
- Every empty state: illustration + specific heading + CTA.
  "No results" is never acceptable as a heading — be specific about
  what's absent and how to add it.
- Every loading state: skeleton matching content shape. A generic
  spinner is not an acceptable substitute for content-shaped skeletons
  on anything that takes more than 300ms.
- Every form field: visible label above the field. Placeholder text
  is supplementary UX, not a label. Never remove the label.

## PKR / CNIC formatting

- PKR amounts: always `formatPKR()` from packages/ui/src/lib/format-pkr.ts
  Result looks like: "PKR 14,200,000". Never "Rs.", "₨", or a raw number.
- CNIC: always masked in display via `maskCnic()` except in explicitly
  authorized admin/verification views. Full CNICs never rendered to DOM
  in any public or buyer-facing route.
- Dates: always "DD Mon YYYY" format (e.g., "12 Jun 2026") via
  `formatDate()`. Never MM/DD/YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD in the UI.

## Motion

- Every animation is functional (communicates state change) not
  decorative. If an animation's removal wouldn't confuse the user,
  remove it.
- Duration range: 150ms minimum, 500ms maximum. Exceptions: the
  certificate stamp (400ms spring) and gauge fill (800ms ease-out)
  are documented in the design spec — don't add more exceptions.
- All animations wrapped in: `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`
  with instant/no-animation fallback. This is non-negotiable.

## What NEVER goes in a Sectoria component

- Gradient backgrounds on buttons
- Shadows heavier than var(--shadow-lg)
- Any font other than var(--font-sans) and var(--font-mono)
- Inline color or spacing values (see token discipline above)
- Fear-based microcopy ("Only 2 left!", "Act now!")
- More than 2 primary-weight CTAs visible at once
- A toast for any action involving money — use a confirm dialog
